#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119527 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "71c540ac-3cb5-5e0e-9b7b-5e2ccf1615c5" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown in a 3x3 Linear System Using Cramer's Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "41a8466d-8bc6-5811-a557-e5a4e840f51c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4577" :question/problem "Let $\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ be the solution of the system of equations below. Find $z$.\n${x - y + 3z =-1; 4x - 2z = 1; 3x + y - z =-2$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the system to reveal the coefficients:\n\n$$\n{1x- 1y + 3z =-1; 4x + 0y- 2z = 1; 3x + 1y- 1z =-2\n$$\n\nWe start by finding the determinant of the coefficients matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} &= 1 \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}- (-1) \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix}+ 3 \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= (0 - (-2)) + (-4 + 6) + 3(4 - 0) \\\\\n&= 2 + 2 + 12 \\\\\n&= 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find $D_z$, we take the coefficients matrix, replace the *third* column by the right-hand side of the system, and calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD_{z} =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} &= 1 \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}- (-1) \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+ (-1) \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= (0 - 1) + (-8 - 3) - (4 - 0) \\\\\n&=-1 - 11 - 4 \\\\\n&=-16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying Cramer's rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz &= \\frac{D_{z}}{D} = \\frac{-16}{16} =-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119529 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7263e25b-117e-528c-b2ad-2624f325da35" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Coefficient in a 3x3 Linear System Using Cramer's Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "e1eb00d6-77fe-5110-8095-a54e63981c09" :question/math-academy-id "e-4579" :question/problem "Let $\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ be the solution of the system of equations below. Given that $x = 2$, find the value of $k$.\n${x + 2y - z =-1; kx + 3y - 2z = 1; 3x - y + 2z = 3$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the determinant of the coefficients matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ k & 3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} &= 1 \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}- 2 \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}k & -2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+ (-1) \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}k & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= (6 - 2) - 2(2k + 6) - (-k - 9) \\\\\n&= 4 - 4k - 12 + k + 9 \\\\\n&= 1 - 3k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $x$ is given by\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{D_x}{D}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD_{x} =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} &= (-1) \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}- 2 \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+ (-1) \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= - (6 - 2) - 2(2 + 6) - (-1 - 9) \\\\\n&=-4 - 16 + 10 \\\\\n&=-10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{D_x}{D} = \\dfrac{-10}{1-3k}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we know that $x=2$. So, we can solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{-10}{1 - 3k} &= 2 \\\\\n- 10 &= 2 - 6k \\\\\n6k &= 12 \\\\\nk &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119591 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9c2dc67-95ca-52f2-a292-32891000b76a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parts of Cramer's Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "33693f3b-713f-5ffc-bd4e-27ad7a1e0c7c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6172" :question/problem "Consider the system of equations\n${9x + 3y - 6z = 8; - 6x + 4y + 2z = 3; 5x - 5y - 15z = 10$.\nAccording to Cramer's rule, $x=\\dfrac{D_x}{D}$, where $D$ is the determinant of the coefficient matrix and $D_x=$" :question/worked-solution "We are given the system\n\n$$\n{9x + 3y + (-6)z = 8; (-6)x + 4y + 2z = 3; 5x + (-5)y + (-15)z = 10\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}9 & 3 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -5 & -15\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, to construct $D_x$, we replace the first column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 10 & -5 & -15\\end{vmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120367 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "71c540ac-3cb5-5e0e-9b7b-5e2ccf1615c5" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown in a 3x3 Linear System Using Cramer's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ea7e94b-6323-5b86-897a-329112c25f25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118678} {:answer/id #uuid "a3f39b48-69cd-5596-bcd4-cf8bdf6b1004" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118679} {:answer/id #uuid "cafb42d5-80be-546b-8219-b7845f04f276" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118680} {:answer/id #uuid "4eaab1bf-79cd-5fa5-9900-5401519d718c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118681} {:answer/id #uuid "6d48d72d-27ed-5313-964a-426e0e5770dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118682}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cafb42d5-80be-546b-8219-b7845f04f276" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118680} :answer-field/id #uuid "d000a6eb-dcad-5232-9f34-3f7db78c59cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118677}] :question/id #uuid "1cf1a77a-780e-5431-8f91-cd363218bcc7" :question/mat